Come, come. That is not enough.	來吧，來吧，這樣可不夠。
I am most sorry. The mirrors I have left are all promised to ladies of the Sultan's harem.	深感抱歉，我剩下的鏡子都已許給蘇丹後宮的女眷了。
Certainly! I have a nice mirror for only 2 dirhams.	當然！我這兒有一面上好的鏡子，只要兩枚銀幣。
I have the finest pottery in the city. Nowhere else will you find such quality.	我這裡有全城最好的陶器，別處絕找不到這等品質。
I have the finest pottery anywhere.	我這裡的陶器，天下無雙。
I deal in earthenware -- bowls and plates and jugs and vases for everyday use, but I have richer wares which are not safe to display on this street.	我經營陶器——日常用的碗、盤、罐、瓶，但我還有更貴重的貨品，不便擺在這條街上展示。
It is strange, is it not, that fish taste fair but smell foul.	說來也奇怪，魚吃起來鮮美，聞起來卻臭。
Ali will always find something to complain about.	阿里總能找到抱怨的理由。
It is latin for pottery, more or less.	這大致上是拉丁文，意思是陶器。
A curse upon all thieves!	願詛咒降臨在所有竊賊身上！
I have little to say about that.	這事我沒什麼好說的。
You joke with me. I would not sully my hands with fish.	你在跟我開玩笑吧。我才不會弄髒了手去碰魚。
Two doors down you will find grain.	往前兩間店，就能找到賣穀物的。
Why would you want to carry around expensive table wares? Concentrate on what you truly need.	你何必帶著昂貴的餐具到處走？把心思放在真正需要的東西上吧。
It would be a bulky thing to carry about and of no great use. Concentrate on what you truly need.	這東西又笨重又沒什麼用處。把心思放在真正需要的東西上吧。
I sell only plates, jars, vases, jugs, bowls, goblets, mirrors, and such items.	我只賣盤子、罈子、花瓶、罐子、碗、高腳杯、鏡子這類東西。
Ummm...of what use are these to me?	嗯……這些東西對我有什麼用？
If you wish to buy, I am at your service. If not, go away.	你若要買，我隨時效勞。不買的話，就請離開。
One moment, habibi.	稍候片刻，朋友。
Here you are, a very fine mirror.	給您，這是一面上好的鏡子。
He is bent from a lifetime of toil at sea, and burdened with an injury.	他因一輩子在海上勞碌而佝僂著背，還帶著一身舊傷。
The two baskets on his counter are full of fish.	他櫃檯上的兩個籃子裡裝滿了魚。
The fishmonger is selling fish, of course.	這魚販自然是在賣魚。
I see no jars in the fishmonger's shop.	魚販的攤子上沒有罈子。
The cat, in typical cat fashion, ignores you.	這隻貓一如貓的習性，對你不理不睬。
It is so well fed by the fishmonger that it utterly ignores you.	魚販把牠餵得肥肥胖胖，牠對你完全不屑一顧。
That is not the behaviour of a king.	這可不是身為君王該有的舉止。
Forget the cat. You have things to do.	別管那隻貓了，你還有正事要辦。
Fish for sale! St. Peter's Fish and carp.	賣魚囉！聖彼得魚與鯉魚！
I want only to sell my fish in peace without the snarls and threats of my neighbors, Ali and Fawaz. They complain that my fish stink, that the wind blows the smell into their shops.	我只想安安穩穩地賣我的魚，不必聽鄰居阿里和法瓦茲的咆哮與威脅。他們老抱怨我的魚臭，說風把味道吹進了他們的店裡。
What can I do?! Fish smell like fish. Am I to change the very nature of life itself, or command the wind not to blow?	我能怎麼辦？！魚聞起來就是有魚味。難道要我改變生命本身的天性，或是命令風不要吹嗎？
I will be a happy man only when they leave me in peace.	只有他們不再來煩我，我才能快活。
Thank you for the herbs, habibi. You are not bad, for a foreigner.	謝謝你送的藥草，朋友。以一個外邦人來說，你人還不錯。
I can see you are not truly interested in buying fish.	看得出來，你並非真心想買魚。
Why on earth would you want to carry about a raw, smelly fish?	你到底為什麼想帶著一條生腥的魚到處走？
Try Farabi next door.	去試試隔壁的法拉比。
Did you not notice Ali next door?	你沒注意到隔壁的阿里嗎？
I would direct you next door to Ali, but he is upset over the smell of my fish filling his shop, that he has virtually ceased to do business.	我本該叫你去隔壁找阿里，但他為了我魚攤的臭味瀰漫他店裡而動怒，生意都快做不下去了。
No, no, my cat is not for sale.	不、不，我的貓不賣。
He is useful enough and I always have scraps he can eat.	牠挺有用的，而且我總有些魚雜碎能餵牠。
I have sold fish here all my life, and my father and his fathers before him.	我一輩子都在這裡賣魚，我父親和他的父輩也都是如此。
Ah, it happened long ago. I wrestled with a shark and lost.	啊，那是很久以前的事了。我跟一條鯊魚搏鬥，輸了。
He sells good pots and is honest enough, as merchants go.	他賣的陶器不錯，以商人來說也算誠實。
As you can see, he sells grain.	你也看到了，他賣的是穀物。
Ali refuses to sell me grain any more! He does nothing but complain about the smell of my fish.	阿里再也不肯賣穀物給我了！他成天只會抱怨我魚的臭味。
They make my neighbors more friendly and will surely improve my business.	這些東西能讓我的鄰居們友善些，想必也會讓我的生意好轉。
The word has always been there above my door. It has something to do with fish, I suppose.	那個字一直掛在我門上。我想大概是跟魚有關吧。
I hate thieves. They prey upon me because of my injured leg.	我痛恨竊賊。他們就因為我腿有傷，才敢欺負我。
I am sorry, habibi, but I cannot help you.	抱歉，朋友，這事我幫不了你。
So? What does this mean to me? They do smell fragrant, I must admit.	那又如何？這跟我有什麼關係？不過我得承認，它們聞起來確實芬芳。
No, no, I do not want it.	不、不，我不要這個。
He looks like an ordinary Arab merchant.	他看起來像個尋常的阿拉伯商人。
I am most grateful to you for giving Hayyam the herbs. That smell of fish was driving me mad!	多謝你把藥草送給哈耶姆。那股魚腥味都快把我逼瘋了！
I have the best grain in all of Jerusalem. How may I serve you?	我這裡有全耶路撒冷最好的穀物。有什麼能為您效勞的？
This man is so agitated, he will not stand still long enough to talk to you.	這人焦躁不安，一刻也靜不下來跟你說話。
You cannot even speak to him, let alone give him money.	你連跟他說話都辦不到，更別提給他錢了。
Return when you know what you want.	想清楚要買什麼再回來吧。
You are carrying around quite enough as it is.	你身上帶的東西已經夠多了。
I must have at least 3 fals, since the bag alone is worth 2 coins. The grain is 1 fals for 1 scoop.	我至少得收三枚銅幣，光是袋子就值兩枚錢幣了。穀物是一勺一枚銅幣。
He will not stop to talk.	他不肯停下來說話。
You will have no luck with him.	跟他打交道，你不會有什麼運氣。
If he would ever stand still, you might be able to do business with him.	若他哪天肯站定不動，你或許就能跟他做成生意。
One scoop of grain for 1 fals.	一勺穀物，一枚銅幣。
One scoop of grain for 1 fals, 2 fals for the bag to carry it in, since I see you have not brought a bag for grain.	一勺穀物一枚銅幣，裝穀物的袋子再加兩枚銅幣——我看你沒帶裝穀物的袋子來。
I only sell the bags with the grain. Grain is my business, not bags.	我只在買穀物時才附袋子。我做的是穀物生意，不是賣袋子。
I bought these grain jars from the pottery merchant. Paid too much for them, that is what I think.	這些裝穀物的罈子，是我跟陶器商買的。我看是買貴了。
Those herbs had better work for a long time. I will go mad if I have to smell his fish again.	這些藥草最好能撐久一點，若再讓我聞到他那魚腥味，我會發瘋的。
Eh? Why do you ask me this?	嗯？你為何這麼問我？
You are a most strange man. I have all these jars full of grain, why should I want grain from you?	你這人真是奇怪。我這裡的罈子都裝滿了穀物，我何必跟你要穀物？
No, thank you.	不用了，謝謝。
Here is your grain.	這是你的穀物。
"Lucernae" means "lamps". As for the Greek underneath, I cannot translate it. My Greek is too long out of use.	「Lucernae」的意思是「燈」。至於下面那行希臘文，我譯不出來，我的希臘文荒廢太久了。
Across the street are several merchants.	街對面有幾位商人。
You should know the lamp shop well enough and the Seeress is still with her apples.	你對這間燈鋪應該已經很熟了，那位女預言家也還在賣她的蘋果。
There is the lamp shop. And you now know that the old woman is far more than she appears to be.	那是燈鋪。而你如今也知道，那位老婦人絕非表面看來那麼簡單。
Upon the left is a lamp shop. The old woman on the right is selling apples.	左邊是一間燈鋪，右邊的老婦人在賣蘋果。
"Lucernae" means that he sells lamps.	「Lucernae」意思是他賣燈。
They are ceramic, made in the yoni style. When filled with oil, a flame will burn in the lamp's spout.	這些是陶製的，做成陰形的樣式。注滿油後，燈嘴便會燃起火焰。
The yoni style is...ahem...well, shaped after a certain part of a woman.	這陰形樣式……咳……嗯，是仿照女子身上某個部位做成的。
It looks like an ordinary, well-made broom.	這看起來是把做工精良、卻很尋常的掃帚。
He has a bundle of sweetly pungent herbs hanging in the doorway.	他門口掛著一束氣味濃郁香甜的藥草。
Aye, he has charcoal for sale.	是的，他有炭在賣。
I suppose some of these jars contain various scented oils for the lamps, though some of the vases seem to be purely decorative.	我想這些罈子裡有些裝著供燈用的各式香油，不過有些花瓶似乎純粹是擺設。
On the lamp merchant's shelves are jars, bottles, and vases.	燈鋪商人的架上擺著罈子、瓶子與花瓶。
They are finely made, perhaps designed to hold ground herbs or spices or perfumed oils.	它們做工精細，或許是用來盛裝磨碎的藥草、香料或是香油。
It looks quite ordinary.	看起來相當尋常。
The old woman's basket is filled with the small green apples common to this area.	老婦人的籃子裡裝滿了這一帶常見的小青蘋果。
Do as she instructs. That is the best advice I can give you.	照她的指示去做。這是我能給你最好的建議。
You are meant to find her by your own devices.	你得靠自己找到她。
You surely do not refer to the old woman selling apples? It has been many decades since she was a girl.	你該不會是指那位賣蘋果的老婦人吧？她做姑娘的時候，都已是幾十年前的事了。
Your wisdom and character are being tested. Act wisely.	你的智慧與品格正受到考驗，行事務必明智。
He appears to be a typical, prosperous Arab merchant.	他看起來是個典型、生意興隆的阿拉伯商人。
The lamp shop appears to carry quite a few other items besides oil lamps.	這間燈鋪除了油燈之外，似乎還賣不少別的東西。
I am most pleased to see such a fine customer return. I am at your service.	很高興見到您這樣的貴客再度光臨，我隨時為您效勞。
'Tis odd. I feel I must tell you that although I am content with my lot, for I am an honest man, I would be happier still if my cousin, Achmed, had not broken his oath to me and thus ruined our friendship.	說來奇怪，我覺得該告訴您——雖然我這人誠實，也安於自己的境況，但若我表弟阿賀密德當初沒有背棄誓言、毀了我們的情誼，我會更快活些。
Let us not dwell on Achmed the Oathbreaker. My wares include lamps, oil, vases, jars, bottles, herbs and charcoal. How may I serve you?	別再提那背信的阿賀密德了。我的貨品有燈、油、花瓶、罈子、瓶子、藥草和炭。有什麼能為您效勞的？
Please do not hesitate to ask for something.	您若想要什麼，請儘管開口。
A thousand apologies, but I have no use for a mule.	萬分抱歉，但我用不上騾子。
Have you changed your mind? Well, no matter. Come back any time.	您改變主意了嗎？沒關係，歡迎隨時再來。
I am sorry, I must not have been listening properly. What did you wish to purchase?	抱歉，我方才想必沒聽仔細。您想買什麼？
Forgive a humble merchant, habib, but this is not enough for what you wish to buy.	請恕小商人失禮，朋友，但這些錢不夠買您想要的東西。
But, habib, I sold it to you. What sort of merchant would I be to take it back?	可是，朋友，這東西我已經賣給您了。若又收回，我還算什麼商人？
Surely you need it more than I.	您想必比我更需要它。
Some of these herbs are quite rare, but for you only 1 dirham.	這些藥草有些相當珍稀，不過看在您的份上，只要一枚銀幣。
I sold you the last bundle.	最後一束，我已經賣給您了。
A good-sized bag of charcoal is only 2 fals.	一大袋炭，只要兩枚銅幣。
It is messy stuff to carry about. Once is enough.	這東西帶著到處走挺髒的，一次就夠了。
But you took the only broom, habibi.	但那把唯一的掃帚，您已經拿走了，朋友。
I am not fooled. I saw you speaking with my cousin, Achmed the Oathbreaker, and I can guess what he has sent you here to do.	我可沒被騙。我見您和我那背信的表弟阿賀密德說過話，猜得出他派您來此有何用意。
You see, Achmed went on a pilgrimage. I could not come with him, which grieved me, so I made him swear he would bring me a holy relic.	是這樣的，阿賀密德去朝聖時，我沒能同行，這事讓我十分傷心，於是我要他發誓會替我帶回一件聖物。
But he did not. He said nothing of it and why should I go begging? Nay, I have not spoken to him since and no broom will he get from me!	可是他沒有做到。他隻字未提，我又何必去乞求？不，自那以後我便再沒跟他說過話，他休想從我這兒拿到一把掃帚！
It is not for sale. I keep it here in plain sight and refuse to sell it because it annoys my cousin, Achmed, against whom I have a grudge.	這東西不賣。我故意把它擺在顯眼處、不肯賣掉，就是要氣氣我那心懷怨懟的表弟阿賀密德。
I had rope, but it was all stolen by a miserable little thief who preys upon this bazaar.	我原有繩子，卻全被一個在這市集裡橫行的可惡小賊偷光了。
Do not overburden yourself with something you do not truly need.	別為了不真正需要的東西而讓自己負擔過重。
They are of the finest quality.	這些都是上等的品質。
Thanks to you, habibi, my cousin and I will be friends again. I admit, I have missed visiting his inn.	多虧了您，朋友，我和表弟又能重修舊好了。我得承認，我挺懷念去他客棧作客的日子。
Achmed promised me that he would bring back a holy relic from his pilgrimage, but he did not, so I curse him as an oathbreaker.	阿賀密德答應過我，朝聖歸來時會帶回一件聖物，他卻沒做到，所以我咒他是個背信之人。
He broke a solemn oath to me for which I cannot forgive him.	他對我背棄了莊嚴的誓言，這事我無法原諒。
She is a strange old woman, but her apples are marvelous.	她是個古怪的老婦人，但她的蘋果著實美味。
Any relic would satisfy me, though a relic of the holy Elzer would make me truly joyous.	任何聖物都能讓我滿足，不過若是聖艾爾澤的聖物，我會真正欣喜若狂。
Why, surely you can guess that it means lamps.	哎呀，您想必猜得出，那意思是燈。
I would do much to get my hands on the thief who keeps stealing all my rope!	我願不惜代價，抓住那個老是偷我繩子的賊！
Perhaps I can interest you in something else, habib. I have many fine wares.	或許您對別的東西有興趣，朋友。我這裡好貨不少。
Of what interest is that?	那跟我有什麼關係？
I am sorry, but I am not interested.	抱歉，我沒有興趣。
Why should I buy back what I am selling?	我自己在賣的東西，何必買回來？
A genuine relic! It is not as pleasurable as receiving it from my cousin, Achmed, but....I will give you 1 dirham.	真正的聖物！雖不如親手從我表弟阿賀密德那兒收到來得暢快，但……我給您一枚銀幣。
No, keep it. I have no need for it.	不用了，您留著吧，我用不上。
Sa'alam aleikem, good sir. You look like a man of taste. Notice the fine craftsmanship of my lamps. My lamp oils are of the purest quality.	Sa'alam aleikem，貴客。您看來是位識貨之人。請看我這些燈精湛的做工，我的燈油也是最純淨的品質。
If lamps are not to your taste, I carry good, long- burning charcoal for your brazier.	若燈不合您的心意，我這裡還有耐燒的好炭，供您火盆之用。
